5 Ways I Tackle Flaky Tests in Selenium + Java + TestNG (With Examples)
🧪 Flaky tests are every QA engineer’s nightmare. One minute they pass, the next minute they fail — and your pipeline turns red for no good reason. 😤
If you're using Selenium + Java + TestNG, here are 5 proven ways I’ve reduced flakiness in real-world projects — with examples. 👇

1️⃣ Use Explicit Waits (Smart Timing Handling)
Avoid Thread.sleep() — it's unreliable and slows down tests.
✅ Instead, use WebDriverWait:
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
WebElement button = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("submitBtn")));
button.click();

✔️ This waits only as long as needed, improving stability and speed.

2️⃣ Implement Retry Logic with IRetryAnalyzer
Some flakiness is environmental. Retrying a failed test once can help without masking real bugs.
✅ Create a Retry Analyzer:
public class RetryAnalyzer implements IRetryAnalyzer {
    private int attempt = 0;
    private static final int MAX_RETRIES = 2;

    @Override
    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {
        if (attempt < MAX_RETRIES) {
            attempt++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}

✅ Apply it to your test:
@Test(retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)
public void flakyTest() {
    // test code
}

3️⃣ Isolate Test Data & Avoid Interdependencies
Flaky tests often share state or depend on execution order.
✅ Example: Don't do this
@Test
public void login() {
    // logs in and sets user session
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = "login")
public void placeOrder() {
    // assumes user is logged in
}

✅ Instead, isolate logic:
@Test
public void loginAndPlaceOrder() {
    loginUser();  // reusable method
    placeOrder();
}

✔️ Each test should stand alone, with its own setup and teardown.

4️⃣ Use Robust & Stable Locators
Unstable selectors are a top cause of flakiness in UI tests.
❌ Avoid this:
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//div[4]/button[2]")).click();  // brittle

✅ Prefer this:
driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("[data-test='submit-button']")).click();

Or, use id/name when possible:
driver.findElement(By.id("submitBtn")).click();

✔️ Ask devs to add test-friendly attributes like data-test.

5️⃣ Tag and Quarantine Flaky Tests
Keep flaky tests from polluting your main test suite.
✅ Tag using TestNG groups:
@Test(groups = "flaky", retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)
public void sometimesFails() {
    // test logic
}

✅ Run stable tests only in CI:

    
        
    

✔️ Run flaky tests separately on a nightly or sandbox job.
💡 Final Tip: Track flaky test patterns over time (via Allure, Jenkins, or even a spreadsheet). Fixing 5% of tests can boost reliability 50%.
